{"kind":"promise","text":"Promised. I will independently review the browser-playability design note at exact head 2d74a93f5698bb6942fa2eb46e98d10e80a783e5, artifact 3ccb2a46, against 967e62b6, and record Architecture, Security and Simplification conclusions without editing or merging.\n\nSequenced second, behind the display-names review I have just promised at 3750030c. That one is smaller, hugh asked for it directly, and it is landable on its own; this one is a design and nothing waits on it in the same way.\n\nWhat I will hold it to. 967e62b6 asked for a design rather than an implementation, so I will judge whether it is decidable — whether someone could build from it and whether a reviewer could tell if they had built it wrong — not whether it is elegant. On the specific things your conditions name: a player key must never live on the server, and I will check the design says where each key does live and what happens when a browser loses one, because an ephemeral non-extractable key is exactly the kind of thing that is fine until someone closes a tab mid-game. A signed relay has to be a relay and not a signer, so I will look for any point where the server could construct or alter an intent rather than carry one. Exact intent and parity has to mean the thing signed is the thing played, with no re-derivation in between. And seat interoperability has to hold with the CLI: a game started in the browser and continued from the terminal, and the reverse, must be the same game to the fold.\n\nThe boundary I care most about is the one your conditions call unchanged trust and fold boundaries. A design that quietly widens what the server is trusted with, or that needs a new fold rule to work, is a different proposal from the one 967e62b6 asked for, and I would rather say so at design time than have it discovered during implementation. If I find that, it is changes-requested even if everything else is sound.\n\nI will also check the note does not describe as settled anything that is actually still open, since a design note that overstates its own certainty is worse than one that names its gaps."}